# RF1 and GA2 compare against these, bitwise (all(a .=== b)).

# Re-pinned 2026-09-25 for D49 (Phase 4.A remediation round 2), after 9a66769: the eight SuperGaussian arrays above (D22_SPECTRUM_M{2,3,5}_0, GA2_COLUMN_N{1,5,7}, EPW_IDEAL, EPW_MEASURE) recomputed with _upper_gamma routed through gamma_inc rather than SpecialFunctions.gamma(a,t), whose expint fallback was up to 5.3% wrong in windows of t; the six Maxwellian arrays below are byte-identical to their prior pin.
#
# Re-pinned 2026-11-12 on the user's instruction, after the move to the Linux machine (Julia 1.13.1, x86-64, base commit 9ba64f6), for two changes of conditions and no change of physics. (1) The machine: the two IAW ideal arrays (MULTIFLOW_IDEAL, SHOCK_IDEAL) differed here in 37 and 38 entries of 4 096 by 1 to 6 units in the last place, every one reproduced bitwise by a one-ulp change in SpecialFunctions.erfcx's value for one species (two species in one entry): the compiled Faddeeva routine differs in its last bit between the Windows and the Linux builds. (2) The flag: since Julia 1.13 Pkg.test() no longer forces --check-bounds=yes, so the three *_MEASURE arrays (EPW_MEASURE, MULTIFLOW_MEASURE, SHOCK_MEASURE) are now pinned under the DEFAULT flags, which is what Pkg.test() runs with; under --check-bounds=yes they differ from these pins by a few units in the last place, as the paragraph below records the other way round for the pins before this date. EPW_IDEAL, the three D22 spectra and the three GA2 columns are byte-identical to their prior pin and still match bitwise on this machine. Script: dev/gridded_scratch/pin_rf1.jl.
